{
  "image_name": "92edb95b1dc7b36bab9ef8eb2b05979b.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes three kinds of redox-driven molecular motion in mechanically interlocked molecules. Overall, it shows how changing oxidation state can switch the position, orientation, or size of a ring-like component relative to a larger molecular framework.\n\nIn the top left, a “shuttling” process is illustrated: a macrocycle moves between two recognition sites on a linear axle. Oxidation and reduction, indicated with a Ru-based photosensitizer, oxygen, and a sacrificial donor, appear to shift the ring from a blue, electron-rich state to a purple, more oxidized state at the opposite site.\n\nThe top right presents “rotating,” where a triangular cyclic structure cycles through green, purple, and red states. Electron-transfer steps of different sizes are marked, and the ring seems to rotate or relocate around the triangular path as its charge changes, with oxidation states such as 6+, 3+, and 8+ indicated.\n\nThe bottom panel shows “expansion/contraction” in a larger flexible assembly. On the left, the reduced blue form is expanded into a broad triangular shape, while on the right, the oxidized purple form is more compact and folded. Applied potentials around +1.0 V and −0.7 V drive the reversible switching between these two conformations.",
